  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "Texas Comptroller Don Huffines announced a ban on using state grant money administered by his office to purchase or contract for Flock cameras, ALPRs, and similar vehicle-identification systems, citing Fourth Amendment concerns. The move follows Gov. Greg Abbott's August order pausing state agency funding for Flock cameras amid misuse scandals, including a Lufkin officer charged with 100 counts of misusing Flock data.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The government has no business tracking where law-abiding Texans drive. Doing it without a warrant is a clear violation of the Fourth Amendment of the Constitution",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "Going forward, every grant issued through the Texas Comptroller of Public Accounts will include this restriction, written directly into solicitations and agreements for anyone applying for state funding.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Motor Vehicle Crime Prevention Authority, led by a board mostly appointed by Abbott, had helped state and local agencies install at least 3,200 Flock cameras since 2023.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    }
  ],
